WEBVTT
TITLE Dynamic Memory and Program Layout: malloc, calloc, realloc, free and Where the Bytes Live — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Every C object has one of four storage durations: automatic, static, allocated, or thread.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Each duration is implemented in a region of the process image: text, data, bss, heap and stack.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.000
A local int lives on the stack; a global int lives in data or bss; a malloc block lives on the heap.

4
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Static objects are zeroed before main; automatic and allocated objects start with whatever bytes were there.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.000
The allocator does not hand you n bytes; it hands you a chunk that contains n bytes plus a header.

6
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Chunks are rounded up to a multiple of 16 and never smaller than 32 bytes.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Ask for 1 byte and you get a 32-byte chunk with 24 usable bytes; ask for 25 and you get 48 with 40 usable.

8
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Using the spare usable bytes is legal on glibc and is not portable C; only n bytes are yours by the standard.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.000
malloc returns uninitialised bytes. Whatever was in that memory before is what you will read.

10
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:16.000
calloc takes two arguments and zeroes every byte it returns.

11
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.000
All-bits-zero is zero for integers, but the standard does not promise it means 0.0 or a null pointer.

12
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.000
malloc(0) may return NULL or a unique pointer you must not dereference. The behaviour is implementation-defined.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:38.000
realloc asks for a new size for a block you already own.

14
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.000
If the block can grow in place, the address does not change and nothing is copied.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.000
If it cannot grow in place, realloc allocates elsewhere, copies the smaller of the two sizes, and frees the old block.

16
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:00.000
If it fails, realloc returns NULL and does not free the old block, so p = realloc(p, n) leaks on failure.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.000
free returns the chunk to the allocator. It does not zero the bytes and it does not change your pointer.

18
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:16.000
The released chunk goes on a free list, so the next allocation of that size may get the same address.

19
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.000
After free, the pointer still holds the old address: it dangles, and reading through it is undefined behaviour.

20
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:30.000
GATE CSE 2000 Q1.12 asks exactly this: m = malloc(5); m = NULL is lost memory, free(n) then n->value is dangling.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Leak: the block is still allocated but no pointer to it survives.

22
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Use after free: the block is gone but the pointer is used. The result is undefined, not necessarily a crash.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:53.000
Double free and freeing a pointer you did not get from the allocator are both undefined behaviour.

24
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.000
GATE CSE 2001 Q2.18: returning the address of a local, or writing through an uninitialised pointer, are the faults; the malloc version is fine.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Growing an array by one each time costs 1 + 2 + 3 + ... copies, which is quadratic.

26
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Doubling the capacity makes the copies 1, 2, 4, 8, ..., whose total is below 2n.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Appending 16 items from empty copies 15 elements in total, and 17 items copy 31.

28
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.000
So the average cost per append is a constant, even though one append can cost O(n).

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:38.000
malloc(sizeof(p)) allocates the size of a pointer, not of the object it points at.

30
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:45.000
n * sizeof(int) is computed in size_t. If n is 2^30 on a 32-bit size_t, the product is 2^32, which wraps to 0.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:53.000
The fix is a division check before the multiplication, or calloc, which performs the check itself.

32
00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:00.000
A string of length L needs L + 1 bytes: the terminator is a byte too.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Six chunks of 32 bytes, all allocated, fill a 192-byte region.

34
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Freeing the second, fourth and sixth leaves three holes of 32 bytes: 96 free bytes in total.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:23.000
A request for 40 bytes needs a 48-byte chunk, and no hole is that big, so the allocator takes new memory.

36
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
So the number that matters is the largest free hole, not the total free bytes.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Five questions decide almost every heap question: who owns it, when is it freed, is it initialised, is the result checked, does anything outlive the block.

38
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:45.000
The standard guarantees n bytes, alignment for any type, NULL on failure, and nothing about where they are.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:53.000
The heap is also an operating-system object: malloc asks the kernel for pages with sbrk or mmap when its own region runs out.

40
00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:00.000
A block is a lifetime, not a variable: the pointer is yours, the bytes are borrowed, and the address is never guaranteed.

